INT. ROLLER RINK - SNACK COUNTER - NIGHT

A wood-floored barn with a mirror ball turning slow. Neon palm trees on the walls. The air smells of nacho cheese and floor polish. A disco anthem thumps under the skaters' wheels.

PETRA WOLANSKA (15, braces, borrowed rental skates laced wrong) sits on the end of a bench, holding a paper cone of fries she isn't eating. Her eyes track the rink.

LEVI NAKASHIMA-BOYD (16, concession apron over a hockey jersey, wheels strapped to his work shoes with zip ties) rolls up on the other side of the counter and leans there.

LEVI
You're not skating.

PETRA
I'm resting.

LEVI
For forty minutes?

PETRA
It's a long rest.

He laughs. She almost does. She scoops a fry into her mouth.

LEVI
Come on. I'll show you.

PETRA
I'll fall.

LEVI
Everyone falls.

PETRA
I'll fall in front of people.

LEVI
Those people are eight years old.

She stands, wobbling, and he reaches over the counter, swings out the gate, and glides to her side. He offers an elbow. She grabs it with both hands and they shuffle out onto the boards, Petra rigid as a plank, Levi sliding backward, murmuring instructions, and a group of small children whips past like startled birds.

PETRA
Don't let go.

LEVI
I won't.

PETRA
You're letting go.

LEVI
I'm not.

He lets go. She glides, three feet, five, ten, arms out, eyes huge. She doesn't fall.

PETRA
Levi! Levi, look!

LEVI
I'm looking!

She laughs, a real laugh now, bright and cracked. The mirror ball throws spots of light across her face, one after another, like small applause.
